wen_config` detail format: \"default {model.name or '?'}, auth {auth}, {n_models} listed models, endpoint {host}, {key} (file claim; key validity not tested)\". `providers` counts only list values. `env` truthiness.\n\nImportant leak test: `env` values are never printed; only \"key set in settings env\". So sentinel in env is safe. But note if a *model name* is the sentinel — printed. So the test should place the sentinel ONLY in env values (and maybe an `apiKey` field) so the assertion \"sentinel not in stdout\" is a real leak check, not a false failure. Right: place sentinel as env value only.\n\nHmm, wait: also `security.auth.selectedType` printed — place another sentinel there? That's printed by design (it's a type like \"qwen-oauth\"), so don't treat as secret.\n\nAlso MCP check reads `~/.qwen/settings.json` too and prints server names — if a server env contains a secret... not printed.\n\n10. Broken `~/.qwen/settings.json` → UNKNOWN \"unreadable: {...}\". Note in `check_mcp`, the same file is read again → UNKNOWN \"unreadable\" row too. So one broken file produces two rows (Models and logins + MCP). Good detail to assert: the script must not crash anywhere, and both rows report unreadable.\n\n11. Broken codex cache: `check_codex_cache` catches OSError/ValueError → UNKNOWN \"unreadable: ...\". Also `d.get(\"models\")` when d is a list (valid JSON but not an object) → AttributeError! e.g. `[]` or `[1,2]` → `d.get` fails → crash with traceback, exit 1 (uncaught exception → Python exit code 1). Wait: json.loads(\"[1,2]\") → list; `d.get(\"models\")` → AttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'get'. Uncaught → traceback to stderr, exit code 1. That's a genuine crash-on-malformed-input bug in the script! Same in `check_qwen_config`: `d.get(\"model\")` on a list → AttributeError. And `check_mcp` guards with `isinstance(d, dict)` for servers but `d.get` isn't used... let me re-check: `servers = d.get(\"mcpServers\") if isinstance(d, dict) else None` — guarded. Good. So MCP is safe, but codex cache and qwen config are NOT safe against a JSON array/scalar top-level.\n\nThat's a valuable test case: \"JSON file that parses but is not an object (top-level array)\" → currently CRASHES (exit 1, traceback). Should I propose it as a test expecting the crash, or expecting graceful UNKNOWN? The user asked for tests to be written for the script; a test that documents a crash would be red immediately. Best: include the case with `expected` = \"status unknown 'unreadable: ...', exit 0, no traceback\" and `can_fail_because` noting the current unguarded `d.get` on a list — i.e., this test is *expected to fail today* and pins the fix. That's honest and useful. But plan mode says report faithfully; I must flag that this case is red against current code (a real defect), so EJ knows it's a spec-first test, not a mutation-only test. Hmm — the schema has no field for \"known-red\". I can put it in `expected` or `can_fail_because` text: \"This one is red against the script as it stands: `d.get` is called on a list…\". I'll state it in can_fail_because plus expected. Actually better to put the caveat in `expected` too? Keep it concise: mention in can_fail_because as the \"mutation\" is \"no mutation needed — already red because…\". That satisfies the field semantics reasonably.\n\nLet me double check `check_qwen_config` with top-level array:\n```\nd = json.loads(p.read_text())   # list\nmodel = d.get(\"model\") or {}    # AttributeError\n```\nYes, crash.\n\nAnd `check_codex_cache`: `d = json.loads(...)`; `models = d.get(\"models\")` → crash for list. Also for a JSON string like `\"hello\"` → AttributeError. Also for a number → AttributeError.\n\nAlso `check_qwen_memory`: `idx.read_text(errors=\"replace\")` — fine; OSError caught (read_text can raise IsADirectoryError, a subclass of OSError → caught).\n\n`check_claude_history`: `sum(1 for e in p.iterdir() if e.is_dir())` — iterdir raises if permission denied → uncaught OSError → crash. Not needed.\n\n`list_names`: `p.iterdir()` inside a symlink-to-file? `p.is_dir()` follows symlinks → False for dangling → returns None → \"absent\". Fine.\n\n12. Timeout test timing: CMD_TIMEOUT=10. If I make ALL default binaries hang (fake PATH with 7 hanging scripts), max_workers = min(8, 7) = 7 → all parallel → ~10s. If only one hangs, still ~10s. So test takes ~10s wall. Acceptable; assert elapsed < 20 and each hanging row status unknown with \"no answer within 10s\".\n\nNote: `--require` on a hanging tool → status UNKNOWN → exit 0 (wart). I'd assert exit 0 today? Better to make that part of the --require exit-code case set. Let me design the --require cases:\n\nCase R1: `--require git` where fake PATH has git exiting 0 → exit 0, row verified.\nCase R2: `--require nope` (absent) → exit 1, row MISSING.\nCase R3: two `--require` flags, one present one absent → exit 1 (any missing fails).\nCase R4: no `--require` at all with everything MISSING (empty PATH) → exit 0. (Distinguishes inventory-reporting from gate-failing.)\nCase R5: `--require` a tool present in PATH whose `--version` exits non-zero → today exit 0 + status unknown. This is the wart. I could state expected = exit 1 (require \"verified\" per help text: \"tool that must be verified present; exit 1 if MISSING\"). The help says \"must be verified present\" → implies verified status required. Ambiguous → spec-first test again. Maybe I fold it as one case with expected = \"unknown status, exit 0 (as written; the help text promises 'verified', so pin what you choose — recommended expectation: exit 1)\". Hmm. For a clean deliverable I'd rather have one case that documents the broken-version case with the exit-1 expectation flagged as a decision for EJ. Since it's plan mode and the ask is a case list, including it with a clear note is the honest move. But note the ask also said \"no code, only the list\" — the list is what I deliver.\n\nI think I'll include it, but keep to at most 2 spec-first cases (the non-object JSON crash + the broken-version --require) and label them explicitly. Actually — risk: EJ asked for tests that would be *green* against the script (readiness tests). Two red-out-of-the-box tests are valuable only if labeled. I'll label them clearly in `can_fail_because` (\"already red — no mutation needed; documents defect X\").\n\nHmm, but maybe better to write `expected` as the *current* behavior for the broken-version case (so the suite is green and the wart is documented in the name), and use the crash one as spec-first since a crash is unambiguous. Let me reconsider: a test suite that's green but pins \"broken --version passes --require\" locks in the bug. EJ's memory says he wants can-fail self-tests and multi-round review. I'll mark both as spec-first, and add a note... there's no notes field. I'll be explicit within the fields. Let me choose: mark the non-object-JSON case as defect, and for the broken --version --require case, write expected as current behavior BUT add in `can_fail_because` \"…if the script later tightens --require to verified-only, flip the expectation to 1\". That's actionable.\n\nHmm, careful about one thing for R1: with fake PATH where `git` is a fake exiting 0 — but the test must not touch the real machine. Using a fake PATH dir means real binaries never run. Good. But `git --version` on real machine is harmless anyway.\n\n13. Fake qwen case: PATH dir containing `qwen` shell script:\n```\n#!/bin/sh\necho \"$1\" >> \"$QWEN_LOG\"   # or fixed path in tmp\nif [ \"$1\" = \"--version\" ]; then echo \"qwen-fake 0.0\"; exit 0; fi\necho \"UNEXPECTED INVOCATION: $*\" >&2; exit 9\n```\nAssert log file has exactly one line \"--version\", and stdout row for qwen = verified \"qwen-fake 0.0\". Also with `--net`: agy fake similarly records; assert `agy` only ever called with `--version` when --net is off, and with `--version` + `models` when on. Two cases (net off / net on) — good coverage of the no-quota guarantee:\n- Case: `--net` NOT passed → no network command run (agy marker has only --version), agy login row = unknown \"offline run\".\n- Case: `--net` passed with fake agy (never touches network) → verified \"N models\".\n\n14. `--json` shape case: parse stdout (must be pure JSON, no leading markdown); keys; each check dict keys == {group,item,status,detail,proof}; statuses ⊆ {verified, reported, unknown, MISSING}; groups ⊆ GROUPS and sections ordered by GROUPS index; item names unique? Not necessarily unique — MCP items are labels, blueprint item includes path. Duplicates possible: DEFAULT_BINARIES dedup via dict.fromkeys. So assert no duplicate `item` among Tools rows.\n\nAlso assert `checks` non-empty and contains a row per DEFAULT_BINARIES name.\n\nAlso assert stdout for markdown mode starts with \"# Readiness tools check — \" and every row has 4 cells (pipe count = 5 per row). And a fake tool whose --version prints a pipe → escaped.\n\nLet me now think about which 12–14 to deliver. Required coverage items (8): empty HOME; broken qwen settings; broken codex cache; key never printed; --require exit codes; --json shape; hanging timeout; fake qwen guard. Extras worth adding: --self-test PASS; --net off (no agy models) + --net on with fake agy; --blueprint missing → exit 1 and valid dir; markdown pipe escaping + 100-char truncation; cwd isolation (project instruction files from cwd, repo not reported) — could fold into empty-HOME; usage error exit 2; non-object JSON crash (defect); dedupe of --require duplicating a default binary.\n\nTotal budget 14. Let me pick 13:\n\n1. `R-01` empty HOME + empty PATH (sanitized env) → full inventory, no crash, exit 0, statuses as documented (qwen config unknown \"no …\", codex cache unknown, skill dirs \"absent\", MEMORY absent, MCP \"file absent → no servers from it\"), and every report path points under tmp HOME/cwd — the real home is never read. That last assertion is strong: assert no row's proof/detail contains the real home path (os.path.expanduser(\"~\") of the parent process). \n2. `R-02` broken `~/.qwen/settings.json` (invalid JSON) → two rows (qwen config + MCP) report \"unreadable:\", exit 0, stderr has no traceback.\n3. `R-03` broken `~/.codex/models_cache.json` (truncated JSON) → row status unknown, detail \"unreadable:\", exit 0, and no other row lost.\n4. `R-04` valid codex cache variants? Maybe fold: dict-form models + numeric fetched_at → reported with count and age. Worth one case since check_codex_cache has three shapes. Include as its own case (`R-04`) — asserts list-of-dicts with id/slug/name fallback \"?\", `len(ids)`, first 6 + ellipsis for >6, fetched_at string → \"fetched_at 2026-…\". Good, mutation-rich (e.g. `ids[:6]` → `ids[:5]`).\n5. `R-05` secret in qwen settings env never printed — run twice (--json and markdown), sentinel value absent from stdout+stderr; detail says \"key set in settings env\"; second sentinel in env with empty value still yields \"key set in settings env\" (any()).\n6. `R-06` `--require` matrix: present → 0; absent → 1; mixed two → 1; none → 0 even when all tools MISSING.\n7. `R-07` `--require` on a tool that exists but exits non-zero on --version → status unknown + exit 0 today (wart note).\n8. `R-08` `--json` shape/contract.\n9. `R-09` markdown formatting: header, section order per GROUPS, 4-cell rows, pipe escaping from a fake `--version` output containing \"|\", 100-char truncation.\n10. `R-10` hanging binary → timeout path, elapsed bounds, status unknown, exit 0; also with `--require` on it.\n11. `R-11` qwen guard: fake qwen logs argv, only \"--version\" ever appears; also `--self-test` doesn't run it? separate.\n12. `R-12` `--net` off vs on: agy guard (no `models` without --net; verified row with fake agy listing \"Fetching models…\" + ids with --net; count excludes the Fetching line).\n13. `R-13` `--blueprint` dir missing → MISSING + exit 1; valid dir with README.md + 3 .md → \"4 sections, map present\". And `--workflows` override honored (row points at tmp dir; default absent path → \"absent\"). Hmm that's a lot for one case; maybe split blueprint and workflows into two → 14 total.\n14. `R-14` `--self-test` → exit 0 + PASS line + no inventory table; and red if check_binary lies (mutation).\n15. Possibly `R-15` usage errors: unknown flag → 2; `--require a b` → 2. That'd be 15 > 14 max. So merge: fold `--workflows` into the blueprint case, and keep usage error as its own or fold usage error into --require case? Let me count final list:\n\n1 empty HOME+PATH isolation\n2 broken qwen settings\n3 broken codex cache\n4 codex cache well-formed variants (fold with 3? keep separate — it's the \"reported\" path)\n5 secret never printed\n6 --require exit-code matrix (incl. no-require-all-missing)\n7 --require vs non-zero --version (wart)\n8 --json shape\n9 markdown shape + escaping/truncation\n10 timeout\n11 qwen never executed beyond --version\n12 --net off/on + agy parsing\n13 blueprint + workflows\n14 self-test\n= 14. Exactly at max. Usage-error exit 2 — fold into case 13? Or into case 6 as a step (assert `--require` with a missing value → exit 2). I'll fold \"exit 2 for a bad flag / `--blueprint` without DIR\" into case 13 and mention in case 6 too. Fine.\n\nAlso should there be a case for the non-object JSON crash (top-level array)? That's a 15th. I'll fold it into case 3 as an extra file step? Case 3 is broken codex cache (syntax error). I can make case 3 setup: two files across two runs — hmm, cleaner: fold \"JSON that parses to a non-object\" into case 2 (broken qwen settings) as second step, flagging the defect there. Actually the defect applies to BOTH check_qwen_config and check_codex_cache. Let me make case 3's setup write codex cache as `[1,2]` in a second run to cover the non-object crash, and mark the defect in case 3. Then case 3 covers: truncated JSON → unknown unreadable; top-level array → currently crash (defect, expected after guard = unknown, exit 0).\n\nHmm, that makes case 3's `expected` partly aspirational. I'll be explicit: \"step A green today; step B is red today — the crash is the defect the test exists to pin.\"\n\nLet me also double-check the `--json` with indent=1 parses fine: yes.\n\nOne more check on empty-PATH behavior: with PATH set to an empty (or nonexistent) dir, `python3`/`git` missing → MISSING rows, all fine. But the test runs readiness.py with `sys.executable` (absolute path), so the interpreter itself is found. Good. Also note: `shutil.which` isn't used — subprocess.run with PATH env; if PATH is missing entirely from env, execve lookups use a default confstr path (usually /usr/bin:/bin) → real binaries could be found! So the test MUST set PATH to the fake dir explicitly, not delete PATH. Important implementation detail to write into setup: \"set PATH to the fake bin dir (never unset it — without PATH, execvp falls back to /usr/bin:/bin and the real claude/agy binaries would be run)\". Excellent — that's a genuinely loadable gotcha worth stating. Also LANG etc. fine.\n\nAlso: HOME override works via expanduser; but `Path.home()` in gather is called inside the subprocess → uses subprocess env HOME. Yes.\n\nAlso cwd: run with cwd=tmp_path/\"proj\" so `project skills`/instruction files/`.mcp.json`/disk_usage come from tmp, not the repo. And assert the repo path never appears in output (leak of project identity otherwise). Nice.\n\nFor timeouts case: env should also ensure no other real binary runs; use fake dir with hanging `claude` and stubs exiting 0 for the rest (or leave rest missing).\n\nNote also that with `--require` on a hanging tool the total run is ~10s.\n\nFor case 5 (secret): the sentinel must be a value only reachable via env dict. Setup:\n```\nenv: {\"DASHSCOPE_API_KEY\": \"SENTINEL-<random>\", \"EMPTY_ONE\": \"\"}\napiKey: \"SENTINEL-TWO\"\n```\nAssert both sentinels absent from stdout and stderr in both modes, and `security.auth.selectedType` value IS printed (proves the field is read, so a failure of the sentinel check isn't a silent \"file wasn't parsed\"). Also assert detail contains \"key set in settings env\".\n\ncan_fail_because examples must be *concrete mutations of the script*: e.g. \"replace `key = ... if any(env.values()) ...` with `key = f\"env={env}\"` (or `detail` echoing `json.dumps(d)`) → sentinel leaks → red.\" Or \"in check_qwen_config change `f\\\"no {p}\\\"` → `f\\\"no {p}, tried /etc/qwen\\\"`\" etc. Each can_fail should be a minimal, plausible edit.\n\nNow, per-case, I must ensure expected values are exactly derivable. Let me write the details precisely.\n\nDetail strings to quote:\n- MISSING row: detail \"not on PATH\", proof \"`{name} --version`\"... wait: for rc is None → Check(\"Tools\", name, MISSING, \"not on PATH\", f\"`command -v {name}`\"). Yes proof is \"`command -v <name>`\".\n- VERIFIED row: detail first_line, proof \"`name --version`\".\n- UNKNOWN timeout: detail \"no answer within 10s\", proof \"`name --version`\".\n- UNKNOWN non-zero: detail \"exit 9: <first line>\", proof \"`name --version`\".\n- codex cache missing: status unknown, detail \"no /home/.../.codex/models_cache.json\", proof the path.\n- codex cache unreadable: detail \"unreadable: Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0)\" (for empty file) — better assert prefix \"unreadable:\" only.\n- codex reported: \"{n} model ids{age}{shown} (cache may be stale)\". With fetched_at numeric → \", cache {d}d old\". With string → \", fetched_at YYYY-MM-DD\". shown = \": a, b\" or with \"…\" if >6.\n- qwen config absent: unknown \"no <path>\".\n- agy login offline: unknown \"offline run: not provable without `agy models`; rerun with --net\", proof \"`agy models` (network)\".\n- dir listing absent: status VERIFIED (note: absent dirs report *verified*) detail \"absent (<path>)\". Empty dir → \"empty (<path>)\".\n- blueprint: MISSING \"directory not found\"; verified \"{n} sections, map present/MISSING: files\".\n- instruction files: \"present: none; absent: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, QWEN.md, GEMINI.md\" status verified, proof cwd path.\n- qwen memory absent: verified \"absent (<path>)\".\n- claude history absent: verified \"absent (...)\".\n- MCP file absent: verified \"file absent → no servers from it\".\n- MCP unreadable: unknown \"unreadable: …\".\n- MCP no key: verified \"no mcpServers key\".\n- MCP servers: verified \"2 servers: a, b\".\n- machine: verified \"{cores} cores; {mem} GiB RAM available; {free} G free on {cwd}\" — proof uses backticked names.\n- open unknowns: 2 rows always unknown.\n\nExit code semantics: `exit_code` returns 1 if a required item is MISSING or *not present as an item at all* — e.g. `--require ''`? argparse `--require \"\"` → ['']; item not in known → exit 1. Another interesting case: `--require qwen` when qwen is present → 0. And note: the blueprint check item name is \"blueprint (/path)\" — `--require \"/path\"` wouldn't match. Fine.\n\nEdge: `--require <dir-name>` etc. skip.\n\nAlso self_test uses exit_code with requires=[fake] → fake row present and MISSING → 1 → PASS.\n\nNow on case 9 truncation: fake binary prints a 300-char first line → detail length == 100 (plus \"exit ...\" wrapper if rc nonzero; if rc==0 detail is exactly the truncated line). And pipes: fake prints \"a|b\" → markdown cell \"a\\\\|b\" (escaped). In JSON mode the detail keeps the raw \"a|b\" (proof of where escaping happens). Nice contrast assertion.\n\nCase 12 `--net` with fake `agy` (never real network): fake agy `models` prints \"Fetching models...\" then 3 lines with ids + trailing blank → expect verified \"3 models: a, b, c …\". Note the code joins ids[:4] then \" …\" unconditionally: `f\"{len(ids)} models: {', '.join(ids[:4])} …\"`. So even with 3 ids, output ends with \"…\". Good can_fail: removing the ellipsis or changing [:4]→[:3].\n\nCase 10 timing: assert 9.5 ≤ elapsed ≤ 25 (lower bound proves the timeout actually fired, upper bound proves it's bounded and parallel). Also assert no zombie: subprocess completed. Also assert exit 0 with --require on the hanging tool? That duplicates case 7 wart (UNKNOWN passes --require). I'll mention status unknown only.\n\nAlso for the timeout case: CMD_TIMEOUT=10 → the fake must sleep longer than 10 (e.g. 60) and must be killed. Does subprocess.run kill the child on timeout? Python's subprocess.run with timeout kills the child (communicate timeout → kill, then wait) — for Popen.run it calls process.kill() and communicate again. Yes, `subprocess.run` kills on timeout. So sleep 60 is safe: it gets SIGKILL'd. Test asserts the sleep process is gone (can't easily). I'll assert wall-time < 25s and exit code, and note the fake uses `exec sleep 60` so the killed PID is the sleep itself, no leftover shell. Nice concrete detail.\n\nAlright. Also should I cover `ThreadPoolExecutor` dedupe of `--require` duplicating a DEFAULT binary → exactly one Tools row for that item. Fold into case 6 as a step (assert row count for the repeated name == 1).\n\nNow, plan mode: the user asked only for the list (no code). The deliverable is structured output. I should also call exit_plan_mode? The instructions say present plan by calling exit_plan_mode when ready. But this run is non-interactive (structured output mode; the system says final answer must be structured_output; first valid call ends session). Plan mode + structured output: the task is purely informational (propose test cases), no file changes needed, so exit_plan_mode isn't necessary — and calling it might block. The interaction mode says non-interactive single turn. I'll just deliver via structured_output. No edits were made.\n\nOne more consideration: should I verify a couple of behaviors empirically with read-only means? Plan mode forbids state-modifying tools; running readiness.py itself would execute real binaries (agy --version etc.) — read-only-ish but the memory note says it was verified read-only before. However running it in plan mode with the real HOME would be a subprocess run — the user explicitly said tests must not touch the real machine, and I shouldn't run the script now (not necessary; and it could spend nothing but is still an execution). I'll avoid it. My claims are read from the code.\n\nActually, one thing I want to be sure about: does `subprocess.run` raise FileNotFoundError when the *file exists but is not executable* → PermissionError (OSError subclass) — caught by `except OSError` → (None, str(e)). And FileNotFoundError is also an OSError, but it's caught first. Yes ordering correct. Not used in my cases except maybe worth a case — skip (14 max already).\n\nLet me also double check `check_dir_listing(..., absent_status=VERIFIED)`: default VERIFIED for both absent and empty. Confirmed the wart: \"absent\" reported as *verified* — which contradicts the docstring's status legend (MISSING = expected and not there). Worth asserting as-is with a note in can_fail/expected. I'll state expected as \"status verified with detail 'absent (…)'\" and note in can_fail_because that changing the default absent_status to MISSING makes it red, i.e. the test pins today's choice and flipping it is a deliberate decision.\n\nAlso: `--workflows` default is cwd/.claude/workflows → with tmp cwd → absent. Good.\n\nNow write the 14 cases with tight, evidence-bearing text. Keep each field reasonably concise but concrete. Use ids like RT-01…RT-14.\n\nLet me draft each:\n\nRT-01 — \"Sanitized HOME+PATH+cwd: full inventory without touching the machine\"\nsetup: tmp_path: `home/` (empty), `bin/` (empty dir), `proj/` (empty cwd). Write fake executables for all seven DEFAULT_BINARIES in bin/ that echo \"<name>-fake 0.1\" and exit 0 (PATH must be *set* to bin/, never deleted — with PATH unset, execvp falls back to /usr/bin:/bin and the real claude/agy/qwen would run). Run readiness.py with subprocess, env = {\"HOME\": home, \"PATH\": bin} plus sys.executable by absolute path, cwd=proj.\naction: run with no flags, capture stdout/stderr/rc/timing.\nexpected: rc 0; stdout parses as markdown starting \"# Readiness tools check —\"; 7 Tools rows verified with the fake version strings; no row's detail/proof contains the real home (os.path.expanduser(\"~\")) or the repo path; qwen config status unknown detail \"no <tmp home>/.qwen/settings.json\"; codex cache unknown \"no …\"; the 5 skill-dir rows status verified detail \"absent (<path>)\" (pins today's choice that an absent dir is *verified*, not MISSING); 4 MCP rows \"file absent → no servers from it\"; instruction files \"present: none; absent: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, QWEN.md, GEMINI.md\"; 2 Open unknowns rows; stderr empty, no \"Traceback\".\ncan_fail_because: `gather()` calling `check_qwen_config(Path(os.path.realhome))` … better: \"change `home, cwd = Path.home(), Path.cwd()` to `Path(\"~\").expanduser(), Path(SKILL_DIR)` …\" Hmm. Simplest concrete mutation: in `gather`, replace `Path.home()` with `Path(\"/home/smoke01\")` (or drop the `home` argument and use the inherited env), → RT-01's \"no row mentions the real home\" assertion goes red. Also removing the try/except in check_machine? Let me use: \"make `check_dir_listing` raise on an absent dir (change `names = list_names(p)` to `names = list_names(p) or []`+`p.iterdir()`), or flip `absent_status` default from VERIFIED to MISSING → the absent-skill-dir rows change status and this test goes red (the latter is a deliberate spec change, not a bug).\" Choose the Path.home mutation as primary, mention absent_status flip.\n\nHmm, the field should be one concrete mutation ideally. I'll give one primary mutation, sometimes with a parenthetical.\n\nRT-02 — broken `~/.qwen/settings.json`\nsetup: home/.qwen/settings.json = `{ \"model\": {` (truncated, invalid JSON). PATH = empty fake bin dir (no binaries → all Tools MISSING; nothing real runs).\naction: run `--json`.\nexpected: rc 0 (missing tools alone don't fail); both the \"qwen config (model, provider)\" row (Models and logins) and the \"~/.qwen/settings.json\" MCP row report status unknown with detail starting \"unreadable:\"; the string \"unreadable\" appears nowhere else; stdout has 4 MCP rows total; no traceback in stderr; the other 5 MCP/… rows still present (a parse failure must not abort the run).\ncan_fail_because: narrow `except (OSError, ValueError)` to `except OSError` in check_qwen_config → json.JSONDecodeError escapes, the subprocess dies with a traceback, rc 1, expected rc 0 and unknown row go red.\n\nRT-03 — broken `~/.codex/models_cache.json` (syntax + non-object)\nsetup: two runs; (a) cache file = `{\"models\": [{\"id\": \"x\"},` (truncated); (b) cache = `[{\"id\": \"gpt-6\"}]` (valid JSON, top-level array). Empty PATH bin dir; HOME set.\naction: `readiness.py --json` both times.\nexpected: (a) rc 0, \"codex model cache\" row status unknown, detail \"unreadable:\" + JSON error text, proof = the cache path. (b) same expectations as (a) — and note this step is RED against the script as it stands: `check_codex_cache` does `d.get(\"models\")` on a list, raising AttributeError → the process dies with rc 1 and a traceback. The test pins the guard (`isinstance(d, dict)` like `check_mcp` already has) — `check_qwen_config` crashes the same way on an array.\ncan_fail_because: no mutation is needed for (b) — it is already red (the unguarded `d.get(\"models\")`); for (a) removing the `except (OSError, ValueError)` around `json.loads` turns the row into a traceback and rc 1.\n\nRT-04 — well-formed codex cache → `reported` row contents\nsetup: three caches in three runs: (i) `{\"models\":[{\"id\":\"a\"},{\"slug\":\"b\"},{\"name\":\"c\"},{\"note\":1},\"raw\"], \"fetched_at\": <epoch 10 days ago>}`; (ii) 9 ids (`m1..m9`) no fetched_at; (iii) `{\"models\":{\"x\":{\"o\":1},\"y\":{}},\"fetched_at\":\"2026-09-01T00:00:00Z\"}`.\naction: `--json`, read the \"codex model cache\" row.\nexpected: (i) status reported, detail \"4 model ids, cache 10d old: a, b, c, ? (cache may be stale)\" — the dict entries fall back id→slug→name→\"?\", a bare string entry is used as-is; (ii) \"9 model ids: m1, m2, m3, m4, m5, m6 … (cache may be stale)\