tionally (or checking only `c.status == MISSING` and dropping `code == 1`) → the polluted run exits 0 and the FAIL line never appears → red; or in `exit_code` returning 0 for any MISSING → same.\n\nHmm, the \"status=verified\" wording: `check_binary` with rc==0 → VERIFIED → self_test prints f\"self-test FAIL: status={c.status}, exit code={code} (expected MISSING, 1)\" → \"status=verified, exit code=0\". Correct.\n\nThat's 14 cases. Good — covers all requested items:\n- empty HOME → RT-01 (plus RT-02..05 with empty home)\n- broken qwen settings.json → RT-02\n- broken codex models_cache.json → RT-03\n- key never printed → RT-05\n- --require exit codes → RT-06, RT-07\n- --json shape → RT-08\n- hanging binary timeout via fake PATH → RT-10\n- fake qwen never executed beyond --version → RT-11 (+RT-12 for net)\n\nExtra: RT-04, RT-09, RT-13, RT-14.\n\nNow: names should be short. Each field must be reasonably compact but concrete. I'll write the JSON now.\n\nI should also mention in my reply text (before structured output — actually the final answer IS the structured output; the tool call ends the session). The instruction: \"In structured-output mode, this is the ONLY way to deliver the final result\". I can add brief framing in the text preceding the call, but it's safest to keep the visible text minimal and put everything in the payload. I'll add a one-paragraph note about the two findings (crash on non-object JSON, --require accepting a broken binary) since those are load-bearing discoveries... but final answer as plain text may be discarded. I'll include those caveats inside the relevant case fields (already done) and also a short preface text. Keep preface short.\n\nLet me finalize wording, careful about quoting (avoid literal braces issues; it's JSON so escape quotes properly — use plain ASCII where possible; the em-dash and arrow characters are fine in JSON strings).\n\nI'll write expected strings with backticks and quotes escaped as \\\" where needed — actually simp